In a horizontal-tb column flex container, Ladybird now measures
baselines across the horizontal axis: an item in a vertical writing mode
by its own baselines, any other item by one synthesized from its left
border edge; and vertical-rl items share a group at the right edge.
That’s what Blink does: DetermineBaselineWritingMode() in
baseline_utils.h gives a horizontal-tb item vertical-lr in an ltr
container, LogicalBoxFragment::SynthesizedBaseline() puts its alphabetic
baseline on the line-under (left) edge, and DetermineBaselineGroup()
puts vertical-rl items in the minor group, which
FlexLayoutAlgorithm::BaselineAscent() measures from the cross-end
margin.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5
(Playwright’s build), all three agree on the test’s rows with real or
synthesized horizontal-tb baselines. On the rows of empty vertical-lr
and vertical-rl boxes, Chrome and WebKit agree with each other and with
Ladybird now, and Firefox synthesizes differently. Ladybird still
differs from all three for vertical text: they align its central
baseline, and Ladybird its alphabetic one.
